{{right|''Cary'', tr. of Dante’s Purgatory, xxiv. 121.}}}}

¶
'''twifold''' (twī′fōld), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''twifold''; < ''twifold'', ''a.''] In a twofold manner or measure. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|Your T beard is the fashion,<br>And ''twifold'' doth express the enamour’d courtier.
{{right|''Fletcher (and another)'', Queen of Corinth, iv. 1.}}}}

'''twiforked†''' (twī′fôrkt), ''a.'' [Also ''twyforked''; < ''twi-'' + ''fork'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Two-forked; biforked.
{{smaller block|But this [shaft] exceeds, and with her flaming head,<br>''Twi-fork’d'' with death, has struck my conscience dead.
{{right|''Quarles'', Emblems, ii. 13.}}}}

'''twiform†''' (twī′fôrm), ''a.'' [Also ''twyform''; < ''twi-'' + ''form''.] Same as ''twiformed''.
{{smaller block|She had there been left<br>A guard upon the wain, which I beheld<br>Bound to the ''twyform'' beast [the gryphon].
{{right|''Cary'', tr. of Dante’s Purgatory, xxxii. 95.}}}}

'''twiformed†''' (twī′fôrmd), ''a.'' [Also ''twyformed''; < ''twi-'' + ''form'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Having a double form; biform.
{{smaller block|The eye of heauen did rowle the house about<br>Of that fell ''twi-form’d'' Archer.
{{right|''Davies'', Scourge of Folly, p. 23. (''Davies.'')}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶

'''twig'''<sup>1</sup> (twig), ''n.'' [ME. ''twig'', ''twyg'' (pl. ''twigges'', ''twygges''), with shortened vowel, earlier ''twīg'', ''twī'' (pl. ''twīges''), with long vowel, < AS. ''twīg'' (pl. ''twīgu'') = D. ''twijg'' = LG. ''twich'' = OHG. ''zwīg'', ''zwī'', MHG. ''zwīc'' (''zwīg-''), ''zwī'', G. ''zweig'', a twig; perhaps, with a formative ''-g'', orig. ''-j'', < ''twi-'', etc., two, with ref. to a forked twig; cf. ''twissel'', a forked twig, from the same source.] '''1.''' A small shoot of a tree or other plant; a small branch; a spray.
{{smaller block|<poem>Take ferules eke, or saly ''twygges'' take
Ye may.</poem>
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 38.}}
We liken a young childe to a greene ''twigge'', which ye may easilie bende euery way ye list.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 205.}}
<poem>Who set the ''Twigs'' shall he remember
That is in Haste to sell the Timber?</poem>
{{right|''Prior'', Alma, iii.}}}}
'''2.''' A divining-rod.
{{smaller block|The latest revival among old beliefs is that in the divining-rod. “Our liberal shepherds give it a shorter name,” and so do our conservative peasants, calling the “rod of Jacob” the ''twig''. {{right|''Cornhill Mag.'', XLVII. 83.}}}}
'''3.''' In ''ceram.'', a thin strip of prepared clay used in modeling a pottery vessel, especially in the imitation basketwork common in Leeds pottery.—'''To hop the twig.''' {{smaller|See ''hop''<sup>1</sup>.}}—'''To work the twig,''' {{smaller|to use the divining-rod. ''Cornhill Mag.'', XLVII. 83.}}

'''twig'''<sup>1</sup> (twig), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''twigged'', ppr. ''twigging''. [< ''twig''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To switch; beat. ''Halliwell.''

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To be vigorous or active; be energetic. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Ewes yearly by twinning rich masters do make;
The lamb of such twinners for breeders go take:
For twinlings be twiggers, increase for to bring,
Though some for their ''twigging'' peccavi may sing.</poem>
{{right|''Tusser'', January’s Husbandry.}}}}

'''twig'''<sup>2</sup> (twig), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''twigged'', ppr. ''twigging''. [A var. of ''twick'', unassibilated form of ''twitch'': see ''twick'', ''twitch''<sup>1</sup>, and cf. ''tweak''<sup>1</sup>.] To twitch; jerk. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|Not one kynge hath bene in Englande sens the conquest but they haue ''twygged'' hym one way or other, and had theyr false flynges at him. {{right|''Bp. Bale'', Apology, fol. 142.}}
<poem>Let rantin billies ''twig'' the string,
An’ for anither mutchkin ring.</poem>
{{right|''Morison'', Poems, p. 78. (''Jamieson.'')}}}}

'''twig'''<sup>2</sup> (twig), ''n.'' [< ''twig''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'' Cf. ''twick'', ''tweak''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] A twitch; a jerk; a quick, sudden pull. ''Jamieson.'' [Scotch.]

'''twig'''<sup>3</sup> (twig), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''twigged'', ppr. ''twigging''. [Prob. < Ir. ''tuigim'', I understand, discern, = Gael. ''tuig'', understand.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To notice; observe narrowly; watch.
{{smaller block|''Mug.'' Gentlemen of the Corporation of Garratt——
''Heel-Tap.'' Now, ''twig'' him; now, mind him; mark how he hawls his muscles about. {{right|''Foote'', Mayor of Garratt, ii. 2.}}
The word seems to have got into English through the ugliest kind of jargon, as in the choice morsel of thieves’ cant “''twig'' the cull, he ’s peery”: “observe the fellow, he is watching.” {{right|''Macmillan’s Mag.'' (''Imp. Dict.'')}}}}
'''2.''' To comprehend; understand; perceive; discover.
{{smaller block|From the sudden erubescence of his pallid, ill-fed cheek, . . . I ''twigged'' at once that he didn’t himself know what it meant. {{right|''Dr. J. Brown'', Spare Hours, 1st ser., p. 306.}}}}

readings.c2s4:
¶
{{smaller block|What is that first instantaneous glimpse of some one’s meaning which we have when in vulgar phrase we say we ''twig'' it?
{{right|''W. James'', Prin. of Psychology, I. 253.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To understand; see; “catch on.”
{{smaller block|Don’t you ''twig?''
{{right|''T. Hook'', Gilbert Gurney, III. ii.}}
“I ''twig'',” said Mick.
{{right|''Disraeli'', Sybil, v. 10.}}}}
[Slang in all uses.]

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''twigger''' (twig′ėr), ''n.'' '''1.''' One who or that which is active or energetic. Compare ''twig''<sup>1</sup>, ''v. i.''
{{smaller block|Twinlings be ''twiggers'', increase for to bring.
{{right|''Tusser'', January’s Husbandry.}}}}
'''2†.''' A wanton person of either sex.
{{smaller block|<poem>Now, Benedicite, her mother said;
And hast thou beene already such a ''twigger?''</poem>
{{right|''Pasquil’s Night Cap'' (1612). (''Nares.'')}}
The mother of her was a good ''twigger'' the whilst.
{{right|''Middleton'', No Wit like a Woman’s, iv. 1.}}}}

[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Twig-girdler (''Oncideres cingulatus'').<br>''a'', beetle; ''b'', point of oviposition; ''c'', girdling of the twig; ''e'', egg.}}}}

'''twig-girdler''' (twig′gėr″dlėr), ''n.'' A longicorn beetle, ''Oncideres cingulatus'', which girdles twigs of apple, oak, and other trees in the United States, producing a decaying condition of the wood fitting it as food for the larvæ.

'''twiggy''' (twig′i), ''a.'' [< ''twig''<sup>1</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Consisting of or resembling twigs; made of twigs.
{{smaller block|Small ''twiggie'' stalkes.
{{right|''Gerarde'', Herball (1599), p. 804.}}
Oziers . . . are of innumerable Kinds, . . . being so much smaller than the Sallows, . . . and requiring constant moisture. It likewise yields more limber and flexible twigs . . . for all wicker and ''twiggy'' works. {{right|''Evelyn'', Sylva, i. 20.}}}}
'''2.''' Full of twigs.
{{smaller block|They [the black withies] grow the slowest of all the ''twiggy'' trees.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Sylva, i. 20.}}}}

